# the argument of republic-overreach.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Socrates against Thrasymachus. "x" ranges over persons.
# "unlike_only(x)": x desires more than his unlike but not more than his like.
# "both(x)": x desires more than both his like and his unlike.
atom	just(x)	x is just
atom	unjust(x)	x is unjust
atom	knowing(x)	x is a man who has knowledge
atom	ignorant(x)	x is ignorant
atom	wise(x)	x is wise
atom	good(x)	x is good
atom	bad(x)	x is bad, evil
atom	unlike_only(x)	x does not desire more than his like but more than his unlike
atom	unlike_only_2(x)	wants to outdo others in practising a skill, doing more than its correct standard
atom	both(x)	x desires more than both his like and his unlike
atom	like_wise(x)	x is like the wise and good
atom	like_ignorant(x)	x is like the evil and ignorant
atom	unjust_wise_good	the unjust is good and wise
atom	just_wise_good	the just is good and wise
P1	S1	∀x ((just(x) → unlike_only(x)) ∧ (unjust(x) → both(x)))
P2	S3	unjust_wise_good ∧ ¬just_wise_good	says thrasymachus
P3	S26	∀x (knowing(x) → unlike_only_2(x))	kind empirical
P4	S29	∀x (ignorant(x) → both(x))	kind empirical
P5	S31	∀x (knowing(x) → wise(x))	kind conceptual
P6	S33	∀x (wise(x) → good(x))	kind conceptual
# The text treats "the wise" and "the knowing" as the same; S35 reasons from the knowing to the wise.
A1	-	∀x (wise(x) → knowing(x))
C1	S35	∀x (wise(x) ∧ good(x) → unlike_only_2(x))	from P3 A1
C2	S37	∀x (bad(x) ∧ ignorant(x) → both(x))	from P4
P7	S39	∀x (unjust(x) → both(x))
P8	S42	∀x (just(x) → unlike_only(x))
# The final step treats sharing a manner of desire with a type as being like that type.
A2	-	∀x ((unlike_only_2(x) → like_wise(x)) ∧ (both(x) → like_ignorant(x)))
C	S44	∀x ((just(x) → like_wise(x)) ∧ (unjust(x) → like_ignorant(x)))	from P7 P8 C1 C2 A2
